ne might have interesting work that  they might be interested in what you do  and then you can set up some kind of  collaboration so you can be reserved but  you still have to be on your toes in  terms of socializing with others another  question from a Rob if you were suddenly  appointed the global science president  and could change anything you wish to  about the current system on a whim would  you or would you leave it as it is  there's definitely things I don't like  about the current science system I guess  if I had this power I would do one thing  and that was to increase funding and  science because I think that alone would  probably make a big difference to these  feelings of competitiveness and business  mindset that we feel it's because  science is in general really poorly  funded and it means that it's quite  cutthroat in some sense there's a lot of  people that want to be in the space and  not many jobs can actually be supported  and the other thing I would do was be to  put a focus on like teaching or  education based jobs in academia so what  I mean is that at the moment a lot of  people are employed in the academic or  university system to do research and  then teaching is kind of this obligation  added on to that we're sort of feels  like that person isn't really interested  in teaching or doesn't want to be doing  that as just this obligation they have  to do to get their research money and as  a result I have had some really poor  lecturers over the year  there's people that you could almost  tell they didn't want to be lecturing  these courses at university they just  wanted to throw some notes at us and get  back to doing their research and I think  there should be roles for both kinds of  people I think maybe those people should  be allowed to spend more time on  research if that research is really good  and I think there should be some roles  for people who are actually passionate  about educating university students and  a subject may be doing a little bit of  research but actually being recognized  as being a primarily education position  I think that would really increase the  quality of teaching that goes on in  universities which i think is the most  beautiful physics phenomena probably  know this theorem and understanding  physical phenomena by looking for  symmetries what is your favorite form of  energy without any justification  gravitational potential energy which is  your preferred theory loop quantum  gravity or string theory well hopefully  these are two sides of the same coin is  there any difference between studying  applied physics and physics well I guess  yes some uni's do offer the applied  physics option and I guess it's just a  more broad it's probably more like an  engineering degree in the sense that  what you're studying is probably more  based around the physics ideas that are  useful in industry like most of physics  is useful in industry but if you're  doing applied physics you might be using  simpler maths  maybe a bit more of a simple physics you  probably don't need to understand some  of the complexities of space-time or  something like that  and what we were learning is probably  for someone who has goals to go and work  in industry straight after they  down there studies which is still a  great thing to do but it's probably not  geared towards the academic research  kind of physics any book or other  resources to study essential physics for  everyone other resources I would really  recommend Veritas Ian's channel here on  YouTube even with like a physics degree  I still learn things from his videos all  the time and he covers really quite in  quite an in-depth way actually  interesting concepts in physics in terms  of books for everyone I know some of the  first popular science physics books I  read that gave me sort of a broad  understanding of what's going on in the  field were books by Markus Qian looks  like we need to talk about Kelvin so  that's something I remember enjoying I  probably recommend did you fail a  subject if yes how did you manage to  succeed I never failed a subject at  university the lowest grade I ever got  in a course an undergrad was a B which  is like around 75 percent at the time I  was distraught by that grade and in  hindsight I probably had way too high  expectations and what failure actually  was and I think the reason I did was  because the people I surrounded myself  with my friends and the people I studied  with also had really high expectations  and they were all aiming to get always  above like 90% and so I probably say if  you failed a course or you feel like  you're doing worse than you Carol then  you want to probably change the people  that you surround yourself with because  it can make a huge difference if you're  studying alone and not doing well try to  find some people to study with and if  you've been studying with people that  are maybe bringing you down because they  don't have the same ambitions as you and  they're not aiming for the same grades  you are then don't do group projects  with them  try to get the people that are aiming  for over 90% and try to work with them  it kind of changes your whole mindset  and your work ethic and everything so  that's probably one of the best things  you can do did you even ask do I really  want to be a physicist when things got  tough if so what kept you motivated this  is something that I've not only asked  myself in the past but I always ask  myself  still I think physics is really hard and  often what you're working towards can  always seem really far away and that can  leave you feeling like you're not good  enough or that I don't know that that  it's not worth it I think in a sense  there's not much instant gratification  and physics so to stay motivated what I  sort of think about is why I'm doing  physics in the first place and often I  might come across like a YouTube video  or something that reminds me and it's  the idea that physics is tackling  fundamental questions and I feel like to  work in physics and to work on expanding  human knowledge is one of the nicest  things you can do because you're not  just working you're contributing in a  fundamental way to like the history of  human life I sort of see it in that way  that if I was to leave physics and do  some other job maybe that I would  consider mundane that's just for money  or that I might be good at but I don't  see any sort of fundamental purpose  behind I feel like I wouldn't be  fulfilled and that I'd want to work on  something as exciting and interesting as  physics I really want to study physics  and change to a stem career but I also  feel quite ridiculous because I'm 41 a  law graduate from Brazil married and a  parent of a child do you think it's too  late for me I've had a few variations on  this question before and it's really  hard for me to  say I'm generally quite optimistic and  the idea that you can study physics at  any age and any background and that you  should try and do what you're passionate  about  having said that physics is hard and if  you're coming from a different  background to other people whether  that's you haven't studied physics for  ages or you've got a busy life elsewhere  you're just going to make it harder for  yourself so I'd always recommend doing  what you want to do but you're going to  have to realise that it may be harder  for you and you should probably think  about what you want to get out of doing  a physics degree so a lot of people go  into a physics degree thinking that  they're going to win a Nobel Prize go  into academia be a professor for the  rest of their life and sure some people  do but lots don't and you can go and  work in industry you can go work and  teaching there's lots of other things  you can do but you might not be living  up to your expectations of where  studying physics would take you so if  you're going back to study physics late  in life you should probably think about  why you actually want to do that what  career you see yourself ending up on so  if you want to do it because you want to  go into the uni system become a  professor and do your own independent  research know that that's probably  you're really quite a long road much  more than the three or four year  undergrad degree if you just want to do  the degree for interest and then maybe  switching to a slightly more stem career  and industry or something else sure  that's fine but just be I guess  realistic with your expectations for  after doing the degree I don't think  it's too late to do the physics degree  at any age I know people have done a  really quite late in life I studied with  them and they were great but you just  realized that the degree might be fine  but what are you going to do  that some of my other questions here are  things like explain quantum entanglement  and detail that's something I'd love to  try and do but it's probably a bit too  ambitious for this Q&amp;A so I'm sorry if I  haven't answered your questions from  Instagram but try and type of reply um  and I really do appreciate the amount of  comments I got so thanks for watching  and I'll see you next time
 

